---
import ProjectLayout from "../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
import ImageSlider from "../../../components/project/ImageSlider";
import ProjectExternalLink from "../../../components/project/ProjectExternalLink.astro";
import SectionTitle from "../../../components/project/SectionTitle.astro";
import HighlightCard from "../../../components/project/HighlightCard.astro";
import { dayblocksImages } from "../../../data/dayblocksSlides";

const images = dayblocksImages("en");
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="DayBlocks | Modular Card Journal"
  tags={["iOS App", "0-to-1 Product", "Product Strategy", "Light Social", "Habit Building"]}
>
  <div slot="aside" class="space-y-6">
    <ImageSlider images={images} client:load />
    <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-2">
      <HighlightCard value="7+" label="Modular Card Types" />
      <HighlightCard value="iOS" label="Solo Developed" />
      <HighlightCard value="App Store" label="Official Release" />
    </div>
    <ProjectExternalLink href="https://apps.apple.com/tw/app/dayblocks/id6761128709" label="View on App Store" />
  </div>

  <SectionTitle title="Three Apps Just to Log One Day" />
  <p>
    Logging a full day often means juggling several apps: a journal in Day One, habits in Streaks, focus time in Forest and photos in the camera roll. A typical target user, a 25-year-old security engineer working in finance, put it this way:
  </p>
  <p class="border-l-2 border-zinc-500 pl-4 text-zinc-300 italic">
    "I write my journal in Day One, track habits in Streaks and log focus time in Forest. Just switching between three apps makes me want to give up."
  </p>
  <p>The scenario breaks down into three problems:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2">
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Fragmentation</strong>: journaling, habits, focus, health and photos are scattered across tools, the data never connects, and every extra app adds friction.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">No social motivation</strong>: most journaling apps are private islands. You can't see your friends' progress and nobody cheers you on.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Rigid templates</strong>: traditional apps give everyone the same layout, no matter what their day actually revolves around.</li>
  </ul>

  <SectionTitle title="Why a Journal Needs Social" />
  <p>
    Integration and custom layouts are intuitive needs. Social needs more justification: journals are private, so why would anyone want others to see them?
  </p>
  <p>
    The answer came from how Gen Z presents itself. Fewer polished selfies, more real data: Spotify Wrapped generates over 2 billion social impressions a year, Strava mentions on Instagram grew 70%, and Duolingo sees over 30 million streak shares a month. "Top 0.5% listener" or "running four weeks straight" says more about a person than a photo does.
  </p>
  <p>
    Research also shows that sharing progress with friends can raise goal completion to 65%. But young users want that without the pressure of comments and likes. They want to be seen, not judged.
  </p>
  <p class="border-l-2 border-zinc-500 pl-4 text-zinc-300 italic">
    If Strava lets your athletic self be seen and Spotify lets your music taste be seen, DayBlocks lets your whole self be seen: not just runs and playlists, but moods, focus and the small wins of every day.
  </p>

  <SectionTitle title="Market and Competitors: Finding an Empty Spot" />
  <p>
    Driven by the digital self-care trend, the global digital journaling app market is projected at about USD 110M in 2026, growing at an 11.5% CAGR. Asia-Pacific accounts for roughly 30% (USD 33M). Starting from Taiwan, with a target of 100,000 users and 2% converting to Pro, the first-year obtainable market is about NT$1.77M.
  </p>
  <p>
    The main competitors are Daylio and DailyBean, both built around lightweight mood logging. Daylio leads on deep statistics and a large user base; DailyBean wins on a minimal calendar aesthetic. Comparing them feature by feature, productivity tools like a Pomodoro timer, task streaks, wake-up check-ins and habit tracking were missing from both, and so were a friends feed and leaderboard. Neither offered automatic weather or freely arranged cards either. That became the positioning: <strong class="text-zinc-200">a productivity journal with pressure-free social</strong>.
  </p>

  <SectionTitle title="Defining Success Before Building" />
  <p>Before development, success was defined with OKRs:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2">
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Objective</strong>: build an app that helps people keep a journaling habit.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">KR1 Feature completeness</strong>: ship 10 card types covering both mindfulness and productivity.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">KR2 Cross-platform</strong>: support iOS and Android from one codebase.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">KR3 User validation</strong>: run a Beta with real users and complete at least one iteration based on their feedback.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Next came a 15-chapter bilingual PRD covering user scenarios, data architecture and UX flows, plus a card-system spec defining each card's sizes, data structure and global interaction rules, with i18n built into the architecture.
  </p>
  <p>Features were split into four priority tiers:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2">
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">P0 Core card system</strong>: creating, editing and arranging cards, and storing their data.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">P1 Streaks</strong>: consecutive-day counts, the buffer mechanism and achievement badges.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">P2 Social</strong>: friends, the activity feed, cheers and leaderboards.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">P3 Future</strong>: music cards, HealthKit integration and AI insights.</li>
  </ul>

  <SectionTitle title="Four Trade-offs" />
  <p>Four of the main trade-offs made during development:</p>

  <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-zinc-200 mt-4 mb-2">1. Light social (cheers) vs. deep social (comments)</h3>
  <p>
    Social interaction is limited to cheers and kudos, with no comments. Completing a task or adding a card automatically appears on friends' feeds, inspired by Duolingo, so nobody has to compose a post. The cost is shallower interaction; what it avoids is the pressure of comments and likes.
  </p>

  <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-zinc-200 mt-6 mb-2">2. Local storage + cloud social</h3>
  <p>
    Journals are deeply personal, so they live in on-device SQLite by default. Only the data the social feed needs goes to the cloud. The backend therefore only carries traffic for the social features.
  </p>

  <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-zinc-200 mt-6 mb-2">3. Forgiving streaks vs. reset on a miss</h3>
  <p>
    Most streaks reset after one missed day, which is brutal for someone just starting a habit; one miss often means quitting. DayBlocks adds a buffer, so an occasional missed day does not reset the accumulated count.
  </p>

  <h3 class="text-lg font-medium text-zinc-200 mt-6 mb-2">4. Smart layout inheritance vs. daily setup</h3>
  <p>
    Cards can be dragged and resized freely, but rearranging every day turns freedom into a chore. Each new day inherits the previous day's layout, adjusted only when needed.
  </p>

  <SectionTitle title="What Shipped" />
  <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2">
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Modular cards</strong>: weather, mood, journal, photo, Pomodoro, habits, tasks, to-dos and wake-up check-ins, plus a Strava run card, arranged on a dynamic grid that sets each card's size and position.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Light social feed</strong>: a friends feed, cheers and kudos, and a friends leaderboard; logging activity appears on the feed automatically, with no separate post.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Data insights</strong>: heatmaps of each habit's completion history, alongside streak flame badges and mood trend charts.</li>
  </ul>

  <SectionTitle title="Building It" />
  <p>
    Role: product owner and lead developer, covering requirements, UX flows, UI guidelines, full-stack development and deployment, with 436 of 457 commits (about 95%).
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2">
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Cross-platform app</strong>: React 18 + Capacitor 8, producing iOS and Android builds from one codebase.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Backend</strong>: PocketBase on Zeabur handling OAuth sign-in and social data sync, plus a third-party weather API.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Data and notifications</strong>: local SQLite with hardware-encrypted secure storage, local push notifications and an automated CI/CD pipeline.</li>
  </ul>

  <SectionTitle title="Polishing Before Launch" />
  <p>Device testing and user feedback led to several changes before launch:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2">
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Performance</strong>: removed the expensive backdrop-blur on iOS and switched to preloaded sheets, eliminating stutter when panels open.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Haptics</strong>: added haptic feedback to actions like completing a task or checking off a to-do.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Respectful notifications</strong>: reminders are skipped if you have already logged that day.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Visual restraint</strong>: cut redundant animations and shortened interface response time.</li>
  </ul>

  <SectionTitle title="Beta Testing" />
  <p>
    A Friends &amp; Family Beta gathered feedback that diverged from the original priorities: the photo upload flow and the social panel, neither ranked high, drew the most comments from testers and became the focus of the next iteration.
  </p>
  <p>
    The project went through a full cycle from problem definition, spec writing, development and launch to gathering feedback; the part that took the most effort was not building the features but deciding their priority.
  </p>

  <SectionTitle title="Business Model and What's Next" />
  <p>
    DayBlocks uses a freemium model: Pro is USD 2.99/month or USD 24.99/year and unlocks advanced features. The local-first architecture keeps running costs low, and growth relies on word of mouth and sharing on communities like Threads. The financial plan projects break-even at about 200 paid users in Q1 2027, with a year-three target of 2,500 paid users.
  </p>
  <p>The roadmap has four phases:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc ml-6 space-y-2">
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Phase 1 Launch</strong>: bug fixes, performance, onboarding, push notifications, paywall and App Store release (done).</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Phase 2 Social flywheel</strong>: Instagram Story share cards, streak share images, monthly and yearly recaps, achievement badges, community challenges.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Phase 3 Expansion</strong>: advanced stats dashboard, cloud backup, music card, dark mode and themes, iOS widgets.</li>
    <li><strong class="text-zinc-200">Phase 4 AI</strong>: AI-powered insights and HealthKit integration.</li>
  </ul>
</ProjectLayout>
